NAME
    num - 32-bit counts on a 16-bit int (#include "../lib_num.c")

SYNOPSIS
    #include "../lib_num.c"
    int n[2];                     n[0] = high word, n[1] = low word
    inc32(n);  put32(n[0], n[1], 7);

DESCRIPTION
    y1cc's int is an unsigned 16-bit word, so the bytes of several
    files, or the size of a file of 64K or more, need two of them.
        inc32(c)          adds one to the count c[0]:c[1]
        put32(hi, lo, w)  prints hi:lo in decimal, right-justified in
                          w columns (0 = no padding)
    wc keeps its lines, words and bytes this way; dir prints sizes with
    put32 (the high part from byte 18 of the directory entry).

EXAMPLES
    put32(1, 0, 0)        65536
    put32(0, 98, 7)       "     98"

NOTES
    New in Y1/OS 2026-09-23, from the 24-bit helpers of the P8X wc.c
    and dir.c (dm10, inc24, put24, putsize24: byte arrays and a
    byte-wise long division): two words instead of three bytes, and one
    shared copy instead of two.

SEE ALSO
